 Advisory Committee provided voted 9-0 that reduction of a protein biomarker in tofersen-treated patients likely predicts it has clinical benefit.\nWhat's next: The FDA will consider the committee's recommendations, with a final decision due next month. \nDon't forget: The FDA's fast-track approval process has drawn concern and comment for the way it can put unproven drugs in consumers' hands, and was targeted for limited changes by Congress last year. \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoche/Genentech Discontinuing Trials – International Huntington Association (https://huntington-disease.org/2026/07/09/roche-genentech-discontinuing-trials/)\nciteturn91view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://huntington-disease.org/2026/07/09/roche-genentech-discontinuing-trials/\",\"lineno\":114}); Total lines: 166\nL114: # Roche/Genentech Discontinuing Trials\nL115: \nL116:   * July 9, 2026\nL117: \nL118: Today Roche shared difficult news. After careful review of the data, they have decided to stop two of their Huntington’s disease studies: GENERATION HD2 and POINT-HD. We’ve provided an overview along with further details provided in a community letter from Roche to help explain these recent decisions.\n"
